        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Investigate the effects of microgravity on the fiber properties of the mouse triceps brachii, a forelimb muscle that has no antigravity function.<P>

APPROACH:<br>
Mice (n = 7) were exposed to microgravity for 13 days on the space shuttle Atlantis (Space Transportation System-135). The fiber cross-sectional area (CSA) and succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) staining intensity of the triceps brachii muscle were compared to those of controls (n = 7). The SDH activity in this muscle was also estimated.<P>

RESULTS:<br>
Microgravity did not affect the body weight, muscle weight, or fiber CSA, but there was reduced SDH staining intensity of all types of fibers, irrespective of the muscle region (P < 0.05). Microgravity also reduced muscle SDH activity (P < 0.05). Conclusions: Short-term exposure to microgravity induced decreased oxidative capacity, but not atrophy, in the triceps brachii muscle in mice.
        </div>

